1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,390
Okay, picture this.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:05,010
A city of about half a million people.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:09,330
Traffic jams, universities, a Saturday
night that goes until sunrise.

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,430
Roughly as many people as Miami.

5
00:00:12,054 --> 00:00:13,434
Now try to drive there.

6
00:00:13,474 --> 00:00:14,224
From anywhere.

7
00:00:14,554 --> 00:00:15,304
You can't.

8
00:00:15,748 --> 00:00:18,798
No highway goes in, and
no highway goes out.

9
00:00:19,128 --> 00:00:21,698
Every car you see on its
streets, every fridge,

10
00:00:21,868 --> 00:00:24,198
every bag of cement, arrived on a boat.

11
00:00:24,388 --> 00:00:26,278
Or squeezed into the belly of a plane.

12
00:00:26,823 --> 00:00:30,133
Welcome to Iquitos, in
the Peruvian Amazon.

13
00:00:30,703 --> 00:00:34,573
Guinness calls it the largest city
in the world with no road connection.

14
00:00:35,198 --> 00:00:37,388
So it's a tiny jungle outpost, right?

15
00:00:37,878 --> 00:00:38,148
Nope.

16
00:00:38,538 --> 00:00:41,258
It's a real, busy city,
bigger than Pittsburgh,

17
00:00:41,298 --> 00:00:42,688
and bigger than St.

18
00:00:42,758 --> 00:00:42,988
Louis.

19
00:00:43,643 --> 00:00:45,053
Then it must be on an island?

20
00:00:45,523 --> 00:00:46,293
Also no.

21
00:00:46,843 --> 00:00:49,173
Iquitos sits on the
mainland of South America.

22
00:00:49,663 --> 00:00:53,013
It's just that the mainland around
it is the Amazon rainforest,

23
00:00:53,323 --> 00:00:56,033
and three rivers wrap
around the city like a moat.

24
00:00:56,449 --> 00:00:58,579
So how did a big city end up here?

25
00:00:58,849 --> 00:01:02,159
And how does it actually work,
when everything has to float in?

26
00:01:02,649 --> 00:01:04,819
We'll start with the
rubber rush that built it,

27
00:01:04,929 --> 00:01:08,579
then follow the river that feeds it,
visit the neighborhood that floats,

28
00:01:08,889 --> 00:01:11,579
and end with the road
that still goes nowhere.

29
00:01:12,009 --> 00:01:12,599
Let's go!

30
00:01:16,547 --> 00:01:18,117
But first, a quick question.

31
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:20,997
How do you even start a city
in the middle of the jungle?

32
00:01:21,447 --> 00:01:22,257
You need a reason.

33
00:01:22,627 --> 00:01:24,337
A really, really good one.

34
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:29,510
Back in nineteen oh three, Iquitos was
a river town of about nine and a half

35
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:30,500
thousand people.

36
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:33,760
Then the world went crazy
for one thing: rubber.

37
00:01:34,357 --> 00:01:35,987
Bicycles needed tires.

38
00:01:36,357 --> 00:01:37,907
Then cars needed tires.

39
00:01:38,017 --> 00:01:41,507
And the best rubber came from
trees that grew wild in the Amazon.

40
00:01:41,937 --> 00:01:44,987
So money poured up the
river, and Iquitos boomed.

41
00:01:45,439 --> 00:01:49,969
Rubber barons built mansions covered in
tiles shipped all the way from Europe.

42
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:54,219
And on the main square there's a strange
little building made entirely of metal

43
00:01:54,259 --> 00:01:56,129
plates: the Iron House.

44
00:01:56,724 --> 00:01:59,424
Tour guides love to tell
you that Gustave Eiffel,

45
00:01:59,464 --> 00:02:01,514
the Eiffel Tower guy, designed it.

46
00:02:01,844 --> 00:02:05,234
Here's the fun part: there's
no real evidence he ever did.

47
00:02:05,584 --> 00:02:08,674
It was bought abroad and
assembled here, piece by piece,

48
00:02:08,784 --> 00:02:09,814
around eighteen ninety.

49
00:02:10,662 --> 00:02:13,012
That piece-by-piece idea was everywhere.

50
00:02:13,382 --> 00:02:18,682
One rubber baron, Carlos Fitzcarrald,
needed to get a steamboat from one river to

51
00:02:18,722 --> 00:02:19,172
another.

52
00:02:19,502 --> 00:02:23,492
So his crew took the boat apart, carried
it over a strip of land in pieces,

53
00:02:23,722 --> 00:02:25,292
and rebuilt it on the other side.

54
00:02:25,924 --> 00:02:30,634
A century later, director Werner
Herzog turned that story into a movie,

55
00:02:30,804 --> 00:02:34,414
Fitzcarraldo, and filmed part
of it right here in Iquitos.

56
00:02:34,864 --> 00:02:37,654
Except his crew dragged
a whole ship over a hill.

57
00:02:38,084 --> 00:02:39,074
No special effects.

58
00:02:39,777 --> 00:02:43,607
But the boom had a very dark side,
and it's worth saying plainly.

59
00:02:44,017 --> 00:02:47,727
The rubber was often collected by
Indigenous people who were forced to work.

60
00:02:48,356 --> 00:02:54,676
In the Putumayo region, a company run by
Iquitos rubber baron Julio César Arana was

61
00:02:54,716 --> 00:02:57,576
exposed in nineteen twelve
by an American engineer,

62
00:02:57,616 --> 00:03:00,196
Walter Hardenburg, and a British diplomat,

63
00:03:00,236 --> 00:03:01,566
Roger Casement.

64
00:03:01,996 --> 00:03:04,886
Historians estimate tens of
thousands of people died.

65
00:03:05,527 --> 00:03:08,377
And the boom ended almost
as fast as it started.

66
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:13,267
Back in eighteen seventy-six, an Englishman
named Henry Wickham had carried about

67
00:03:13,307 --> 00:03:15,777
seventy thousand rubber
seeds out of Brazil.

68
00:03:16,451 --> 00:03:19,321
Those seeds grew into
huge plantations in Asia,

69
00:03:19,451 --> 00:03:21,601
which made rubber cheaper and faster.

70
00:03:22,111 --> 00:03:25,541
By the nineteen twenties, the Amazon's
rubber barely mattered anymore.

71
00:03:25,911 --> 00:03:26,701
The money left.

72
00:03:26,911 --> 00:03:27,941
But the city stayed.

73
00:03:30,532 --> 00:03:33,072
So now you have a city of
former rubber millionaires,

74
00:03:33,112 --> 00:03:35,862
deep in the rainforest, with no road.

75
00:03:36,292 --> 00:03:38,882
Which brings us to the most
practical question of all.

76
00:03:39,364 --> 00:03:40,754
So here's the big question.

77
00:03:41,004 --> 00:03:45,454
If you can't drive to Iquitos, how
does a city this size get its stuff?

78
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:47,890
Answer number one: the river.

79
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:50,010
And not just any river.

80
00:03:50,050 --> 00:03:55,330
Iquitos sits on the Amazon itself, about
two thousand three hundred miles from the

81
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:56,110
Atlantic Ocean.

82
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:59,260
That's roughly the distance
from New York to Phoenix.

83
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:03,451
And yet, ocean-going ships
can sail all the way up here.

84
00:04:03,801 --> 00:04:06,271
A seaport, in the middle of a rainforest!

85
00:04:06,841 --> 00:04:08,531
And you won't be alone on the water.

86
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:12,331
The rivers around Iquitos are
home to pink river dolphins.

87
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:14,171
Yes, actually pink.

88
00:04:14,781 --> 00:04:16,831
Locals have all kinds
of legends about them,

89
00:04:16,901 --> 00:04:20,691
and visitors take boat trips just to
watch them pop up next to the city.

90
00:04:21,257 --> 00:04:23,847
Most supplies, though, come
from the other direction.

91
00:04:24,337 --> 00:04:28,747
The closest town that's connected to
Peru's road network is Yurimaguas.

92
00:04:29,277 --> 00:04:32,427
Trucks drive there, and everything
gets loaded onto barges.

93
00:04:32,949 --> 00:04:36,919
From there, it's about three to four
days down the rivers to Iquitos.

94
00:04:37,249 --> 00:04:40,639
Cars, motorcycles, fuel,
beer, building materials,

95
00:04:41,009 --> 00:04:43,679
all floating along at walking
speed through the jungle.

96
00:04:44,297 --> 00:04:45,827
People travel the same way.

97
00:04:46,297 --> 00:04:50,357
Big riverboats carry passengers who hang
their own hammocks on the deck and swing

98
00:04:50,397 --> 00:04:51,887
their way to the city for days.

99
00:04:52,737 --> 00:04:52,887
Honestly?

100
00:04:53,157 --> 00:04:54,367
Sounds kind of amazing.

101
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:56,393
Or you can just fly.

102
00:04:57,283 --> 00:05:00,973
Lima is about six hundred miles
away, less than two hours by plane.

103
00:05:01,463 --> 00:05:04,133
For many people that's the
only quick way in or out.

104
00:05:04,595 --> 00:05:07,385
And flying here is actually an old idea.

105
00:05:07,855 --> 00:05:11,965
Back in the late nineteen twenties,
Peru started an air service to Iquitos,

106
00:05:12,055 --> 00:05:14,125
with seaplanes landing right on the river.

107
00:05:14,654 --> 00:05:16,084
And there is one road.

108
00:05:16,414 --> 00:05:21,324
It's paved, it's about sixty miles long,
and it goes to a small town called Nauta.

109
00:05:21,794 --> 00:05:22,984
And then it just stops.

110
00:05:23,694 --> 00:05:25,924
Nauta isn't connected
to anything else either.

111
00:05:28,553 --> 00:05:30,603
A sixty-mile road to nowhere.

112
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:33,643
Keep that in mind, because
it comes back at the end.

113
00:05:34,053 --> 00:05:37,823
But first, what does being this
cut off actually do to a city?

114
00:05:38,369 --> 00:05:41,679
Being cut off changes everything,
starting with the lights.

115
00:05:42,249 --> 00:05:45,119
Iquitos isn't connected to
Peru's national power grid.

116
00:05:45,746 --> 00:05:48,856
Instead, the city runs
on its own power plant,

117
00:05:49,006 --> 00:05:50,216
burning fuel oil.

118
00:05:50,546 --> 00:05:53,416
Fuel oil that, yes, comes in by barge.

119
00:05:53,958 --> 00:05:57,348
There's been a plan for years to
build a power line through the jungle,

120
00:05:57,638 --> 00:05:59,948
about three hundred and sixty miles long.

121
00:06:00,378 --> 00:06:04,108
It was signed back in twenty fourteen,
and it's been stuck ever since.

122
00:06:04,694 --> 00:06:07,304
And when the river gets
low, things get tricky.

123
00:06:07,694 --> 00:06:11,164
In the big Amazon droughts of twenty
twenty-three and twenty twenty-four,

124
00:06:11,574 --> 00:06:14,034
rivers dropped to record
lows across the region,

125
00:06:14,074 --> 00:06:17,544
and fuel barges heading
for Iquitos got stuck.

126
00:06:18,099 --> 00:06:20,819
Iquitos even has its
own small oil refinery,

127
00:06:20,859 --> 00:06:23,989
about nine miles from the
city, right on the Amazon.

128
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:26,669
But its fuel still travels by water too.

129
00:06:27,119 --> 00:06:31,119
In September twenty twenty-six,
one fuel boat sat stuck for days,

130
00:06:31,159 --> 00:06:33,289
simply because the river was too low.

131
00:06:33,766 --> 00:06:35,016
Now look at the streets.

132
00:06:35,486 --> 00:06:39,836
Because cars are so hard to bring
in, Iquitos runs on three wheels.

133
00:06:40,569 --> 00:06:42,059
The mototaxi.

134
00:06:42,349 --> 00:06:45,479
Basically a motorcycle with a
little covered bench on the back.

135
00:06:45,869 --> 00:06:49,639
One student study estimated around
thirty thousand of them in the city.

136
00:06:50,221 --> 00:06:53,551
They buzz everywhere,
like a swarm of very loud,

137
00:06:53,661 --> 00:06:55,031
very friendly bees.

138
00:06:55,581 --> 00:06:59,011
A ride across town costs about
as much as a cup of coffee.

139
00:06:59,361 --> 00:07:01,131
It's the sound of Iquitos.

140
00:07:03,633 --> 00:07:06,263
But the real star of Iquitos
isn't on the streets.

141
00:07:06,653 --> 00:07:07,503
It's the water.

142
00:07:08,013 --> 00:07:09,803
And the water here has a schedule.

143
00:07:10,436 --> 00:07:13,026
Okay, now the river does something wild.

144
00:07:13,256 --> 00:07:16,006
Every year it rises,
and every year it falls.

145
00:07:16,376 --> 00:07:16,966
A lot.

146
00:07:17,517 --> 00:07:22,337
At the main port gauge in Iquitos, the
Amazon can swing by around twelve meters

147
00:07:22,377 --> 00:07:24,687
between the dry season
and the flood season.

148
00:07:24,977 --> 00:07:26,247
That's about forty feet.

149
00:07:26,537 --> 00:07:29,747
A four-story building of
water, coming and going.

150
00:07:30,406 --> 00:07:31,996
So how do you live next to that?

151
00:07:32,426 --> 00:07:36,076
Meet Belén, the neighborhood people
call the Venice of the jungle.

152
00:07:36,735 --> 00:07:39,465
Some houses here stand
on tall wooden stilts.

153
00:07:39,835 --> 00:07:43,115
Others simply float, sitting
on big logs tied to posts,

154
00:07:43,155 --> 00:07:46,045
so the whole house rises
and falls with the water.

155
00:07:46,592 --> 00:07:48,262
In the dry months, you walk.

156
00:07:48,632 --> 00:07:51,562
In the wet months, from
roughly February to July,

157
00:07:51,912 --> 00:07:54,452
the streets turn into
canals, and you go to school,

158
00:07:54,492 --> 00:07:56,442
or to the shop, by canoe.

159
00:07:57,116 --> 00:08:01,866
Right next to it is Belén Market, one
of the busiest markets in the Amazon.

160
00:08:02,336 --> 00:08:05,266
Fish, fruit you've never
heard of, jungle medicine.

161
00:08:05,536 --> 00:08:08,626
It's loud, colorful and absolutely packed.

162
00:08:09,207 --> 00:08:13,837
Try the aguaje, a dark red palm fruit
that people here eat by the bagful,

163
00:08:14,027 --> 00:08:15,117
or as ice cream.

164
00:08:15,607 --> 00:08:18,637
Locals will tell you it's
the taste of Iquitos.

165
00:08:19,202 --> 00:08:20,432
Life here isn't easy.

166
00:08:20,962 --> 00:08:24,312
Floods, poverty, and not
enough sewers or clean water.

167
00:08:24,802 --> 00:08:29,332
But people have adapted to the river in
ways a city on dry land never had to.

168
00:08:31,858 --> 00:08:35,248
And when Belén throws a party,
it really throws a party.

169
00:08:35,638 --> 00:08:39,608
Music, face paint and clown
noses, right next to the water.

170
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:41,780
So why not just build a road?

171
00:08:42,290 --> 00:08:43,980
People have been asking that for decades.

172
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:50,630
The latest big idea is a highway north from
Iquitos toward a town called Saramiriza,

173
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:53,430
around two hundred miles
of road through the forest,

174
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:56,370
to finally plug the city
into the rest of the country.

175
00:08:56,958 --> 00:08:59,048
Supporters say it would make life cheaper.

176
00:08:59,398 --> 00:09:02,068
Fuel, food, cars, all of it.

177
00:09:02,611 --> 00:09:07,011
But scientists warn that the route crosses
some of the Amazon's biggest peat swamps

178
00:09:07,051 --> 00:09:08,501
and protected forests.

179
00:09:08,811 --> 00:09:12,121
And Indigenous groups along the
way say they were barely asked.

180
00:09:12,679 --> 00:09:14,069
There's a reason they worry.

181
00:09:14,519 --> 00:09:18,329
In the Amazon, a new road
usually means new loggers,

182
00:09:18,539 --> 00:09:22,129
new farms and new fires spreading
out from both sides of it.

183
00:09:22,564 --> 00:09:23,934
It's already happened here.

184
00:09:24,224 --> 00:09:28,024
When a smaller road was pushed north from
the Amazon river toward the town of El

185
00:09:28,064 --> 00:09:30,694
Estrecho, reporters found new settlements,

186
00:09:30,924 --> 00:09:33,254
illegal logging and conflicts along it.

187
00:09:33,678 --> 00:09:39,158
So in a strange way, the thing that makes
Iquitos so hard to reach is also what

188
00:09:39,198 --> 00:09:40,908
protects the forest around it.

189
00:09:41,311 --> 00:09:45,101
So maybe the real question isn't
why you can't drive to Iquitos.

190
00:09:45,591 --> 00:09:48,121
Maybe it's whether the forest
would survive if you could.

191
00:09:48,675 --> 00:09:52,445
So, how does the biggest city
you can't drive to actually run?

192
00:09:52,875 --> 00:09:56,265
With barges and hammocks, with
a power plant fed by the river,

193
00:09:56,495 --> 00:10:00,635
with thirty thousand mototaxis, and with
houses that simply float when the water

194
00:10:00,675 --> 00:10:01,185
comes.

195
00:10:01,826 --> 00:10:04,356
And remember that car we
talked about at the start?

196
00:10:04,726 --> 00:10:08,836
It took a truck, a barge and a few
days on the Amazon to get here.

197
00:10:09,086 --> 00:10:11,176
In Iquitos, that's just normal.

198
00:10:11,773 --> 00:10:13,483
So here's my question for you.

199
00:10:13,773 --> 00:10:17,103
Would you live in a city you can
only leave by boat or by plane?

200
00:10:17,593 --> 00:10:18,483
Tell me in the comments.

201
00:10:18,933 --> 00:10:20,703
And if you enjoyed this, subscribe.

202
00:10:21,093 --> 00:10:23,563
There are a lot more
strange places to explore.
